A||||4||liebowa||Test Driving an '87 Fiero GT Tomorrow - Need Advice
Z||000000||liebowa||04-18-99||06:40 AM||||Please help a future Fiero owner out!<p>I'm going to be taking an '87 GT (Gold) for a test drive tomorrow. I've already looked it over on the outside, and it seems to be in real nice shape.  But, I need some advice on a couple of small things:<p>1.) The car has 82,000 miles on it.  How do these v6's hold up?<br>2.) The car's emergency brake is broken(disconnected?).  I hear that this is pretty normal for Fieros.  Why?<br>3.) The seats are both torn.  Anyone familiar with hom much it costs to re-apholster these?  Are the speakers still in the headrests?<br>4.) Any suggestions on what things in particular that I should keep an eye out for on this year and model Fiero?<br>5.) Asking price is $4000?  If it runs as nice as it looks, this SEEMS like an average price.  Any thoughts?<br>
Z||000001||Ross||04-18-99||02:49 PM||||I have an 87 se and have had lots of fun whith it,but if I were to buy anoter Fiero(Im looking at an 88 Formula 5spd original owner with only 36000 miles on the odomiter.Hes asking 5500 for it!!)I would go for an 88 GT or Formula.Lotus redesigned the suspetion on this(the last)year,and its superior to all other years.                                 The emergacy break thing is pritty typical.                Good Luck!!   
Z||000002||theogre||04-18-99||03:18 PM||||1. same things as any other used car. alot depends on how much the previous owners beat the hell out of it.<p>2. the e brake is either broken, or the back brakes have been modified using front calipers from another car. Both are common, but neither are desirable for a street driven car.<p>3. speakers are in the seat, or the rear post, depending on what options were originally purchased. all sound system testing it the same thing..... play with all the knobs. speaker seats.... site in both seats and play with all the knobs. Fade full rear and then from laft to right to test the seats/rear posts. fade full front, then left to right to test the front. Expect fried fronts if they are the originals. 15 years of sunlight eats out the cones, even under the grills.<p>4. Get VIN and call pontiac. they can tell you if any recalls are out standing on that particular car. visit  <A HREF="http://www.pontiac.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.pontiac.com</A>  for the phone number for your country.<p>5. not a horridly bad price. seen better, seen worse. Expect to be replacing something or other soon. 15 year+ old cars are just like that.<p><br>get the car on a lift and inspect every thing. look for anything that leaks, crushed coolant pipes, etc.
Z||000003||Patrick||04-18-99||10:51 PM||||<br>With all the time zones involved with visitors to this site, I don't know whether or not your "tomorrow" has occurred yet. So if it's not too late...<p>Make sure that the "<b>Service Engine Soon</b>" light comes on when you turn the ignition key (before engaging the starter). And then make sure that this light goes out when the key is turned further and the engine starts. I was so naive when I bought my Fiero that I didn't realize that the previous owner had disconnected the  <b>Service Engine Soon</b> light to hide the fact that there were engine problems. I learned this lesson the hard way.<p>
Z||000004||PaUL||04-19-99||05:21 PM||||the E brake probably doesnt work because it needs to have the recall done on it. The only way to check other than calling 1-800-GM-CARES is to look at the rear brake pistons. If they have a small yellow plastic plug, than it needs the recall.
